    "A": "Research a generic integer radix-sort replacement, initially leaving generation, verification and MD5 unchanged. Start with stable byte-wise LSD passes and one reusable scratch array; use full-width signed-order keys while moving the original int values unchanged. Do not assume that the source's description of values as positive justifies dropping the sign bit. Compare a wider-digit variant only if measurements warrant its larger histograms and possible copy-back cost. Preserve a correct fallback when scratch allocation fails, and retain a low-overhead path for small arrays. If supported by the unchanged build and justified by remaining budget, investigate at most four workers with private histograms, prefix-derived disjoint scatter ranges and synchronization between passes, rather than per-element atomics. Account for allocation, startup and copy-back in end-to-end measurements. Deliver a complete candidate and evidence distinguishing serial and parallel results.",
    "B": "Research an efficient comparison-based quicksort alternative independently of A. First improve the serial kernel: deterministic sampled pivots, branch-efficient partitioning, insertion sorting of small ranges, bounded recursion and a worst-case fallback. Handle duplicate-heavy ranges without quadratic degeneration. Replace random pivot selection only inside sorting; do not change the generated array. Then evaluate coarse parallel tasks or a bounded worker pool on at most four cores, using only facilities supported by the fixed build. Avoid recursive thread creation and fine-grained tasks; choose general size/depth cutoffs from measurements. The shared randnum state must never be accessed concurrently: sorting should use deterministic pivots or private sorter-only state. Complete all sorting work before verification and hashing. Deliver the fastest validated serial or parallel version, including evidence that parallel overhead does not erase its benefit.",
    "C": "Research exact generation and post-sort overhead, providing components compatible with either sorting tree. Begin with local PRNG state and reduced call/global-memory overhead while retaining the original arithmetic. Do not casually replace drand(0, INT_MAX) with an integer RNG result: floating-point rounding followed by integer conversion can change values, and unsigned-to-signed conversion plus abs has sensitive corner cases. Require an equivalence argument, not merely matching four cases. If generation becomes a measured bottleneck, investigate exact chunked generation using jump-ahead for the two multiply-with-carry recurrences, with moduli a*65536-1 for a=36969 and a=18000. Prove chunk-start states, update indexing and concatenated output equivalence; preserve any final global state needed by a retained sorter. Gate threading by general workload size. Also evaluate a vectorizable sortedness check that includes chunk-boundary pairs. Keep MD5 over the complete final native-int byte sequence in order; independently hashed chunks cannot simply be combined. Report isolated changes and end-to-end results.",
    "G": "Integrate only programs and components supplied by the research trees. Keep the fastest valid standalone candidate as an incumbent; A and B are competing sorting engines, not automatically additive improvements. Evaluate compatible A+C or B+C combinations incrementally, within the existing measurement budget, and accept an integration only on its own automatic correctness and end-to-end timing evidence. Check generator equivalence, signed ordering, duplicates, scratch-buffer ownership, pass parity, synchronization and thread-count limits. Preserve stdin handling, relevant error behavior, verification, COMPUTE_MD5_PROBLEM behavior and exact output formatting. Keep tests, oracle, compiler configuration, budgets and target unchanged. Use general workload thresholds, never exact-case dispatch, precomputed arrays or expected checksums. If integration is slower than its inputs, retain the best measured standalone program rather than assuming component gains add.",
    "analysis": "The supplied program implements the quicksort problem; the other contest problems are unrelated. The only measurement is baseline-e1: correct on four cases, with mean end-to-end time 0.069291 seconds. No timed input size, phase profile or build capabilities are supplied. Source-level opportunities are randomized Lomuto partitioning, recursive overhead, shared-state RNG calls, serial generation and the verification/hash passes; these are hypotheses, not measured bottlenecks. ARM64 with four CPU cores and no GPU favors a portfolio of cache-efficient integer sorting, bounded parallel comparison sorting and exact pipeline improvements. At roughly 69 ms, allocation and thread startup may materially affect results. Correctness requires generating the same integer bytes, sorting them in signed nondecreasing order and hashing the final native representation. Pivot choices may change without changing that result, but generator transformations need stronger justification. All candidates must remain correct for every valid input, not merely the four observed cases."
